{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1394813-blood-bond-vs-vampire-blood-as-drugs","id":1394813,"title":"Blood Bond vs. Vampire Blood as Drugs","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-06-07T10:44:33-07:00","id":1394813,"title":"Blood Bond vs. Vampire Blood as Drugs","content":"New to forum, haven't RPG'd in a number of years but getting back into it. I'm a big fan of Revised VtM but this is sort of a version-independent question.<br /> <br /> Recently was watching a few episodes of True Blood for scenario inspiration and decided I wanted to create a subplot where my coterie is asked to put a stop to someone that is selling Vampire blood as a recreational drug (without advertising what it really is). They will need to investigate who is selling, who is supplying, and what happens to users. That last part is the real dilemma as the concept of Blood Bond is far more important in the game. <br /> <br /> I was thinking they might sell it in single drops akin to LSD. Maybe I could house rule that because the supply is so limited and there aren't that many users yet, and there are a number of suppliers, users don't experience a blood bond (i.e. aren't even aware that's a risk). If they do a drop a couple of times a week, maybe it's from different kindred each time, it's not fresh, and they aren't aware of the blood bond, they only experience some weird visions (which are actually psychic flashes from the donor, but they don't know that). <br /> <br /> Any thoughts?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26213-jrichview","name":"jrichview","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26213_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"18"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-07T11:25:57-07:00","id":1394821,"title":"","content":"There have been many examples of this sort of thing through the years in vampire fiction.<br /> <br /> As you mentioned True Blood just straight up used blood as a drug, it was portrayed as an everyone drug that functions as a physical enhancer.<br /> <br /> The TV series Moonlight used processed vampire blood as a drug called &quot;Black Crystal&quot; which gave a person the psychological highs of being a vampire without needing to feed or have powers. Between its powerful mood altering properties and its lack of illegal substances, it was sold only to the very rich for thousands per vial. The down side was the silver used to paralyze the vampires being drained for its production seeped into the blood and essentially gave Black Crystal junkies heavy metal poisoning.<br /> <br /> The Blade TV series House of Cthon used vampire ash as a street drug which conveyed immense strength but gave the user a vampires thirst to the point they might gnaw their own fingers off in with drawls assuming they didn't attack someone else. Additionally the use of the vampire ash taints the users blood so that most vampires couldn't stomach smelling it, much less drinking it.<br /> <br /> If you are going with a psychic drug, you might want to consider a Tremere or Giovanni producer, thus the drug like properties are brought out by a ritual or process rather than an intrinsic blood property.<br /> <br /> In a couple of my games rather than using the vampire blood as a recreational drug, we went with it as a medical drug. If you google the cost of medical drugs, especially the custom stuff it goes into the tens of thousands of dollars range very easily. Vampire blood creates rapid healing, delayed aging, invigoration, and depending on dosage quite the aphrodisiac. Selling this as a treatment to rich people at $50,000 per dose as a monthly health plan works quite well.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-08T11:45:19-07:00","id":1395119,"title":"","content":"I believe there's a lot of setite magic specifically for this very purpose. You can empower actual drugs with blood, among other things. New wave sorcerers are likely to do this too<br /> <br /> On it's own, vampire blood is just really tasty, makes you feel good but not estatic for a short time, and is addictive. It doesn't really have a &quot;drug&quot; like effect. If people aren't consuming a point's worth, it's not even gonna give them ghoul powers. But they're going to be infatuated with someone they don't know.<br /> <br /> If you get a hold of another vampire's blood, you've got some advantages over them when it comes to rituals. If there's a sorcerer within your party with any number of relevant rituals or you're in good with a capable sorcerer, they could stomp a long search. (then again you can just dominate a supplier into spilling the beans if you're playing VTM)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-08T12:19:21-07:00","id":1395126,"title":"","content":"The L.A by night stream had a small subplot where a couple of thinbloods were using thin blood alchemy to turn vitae into a type of drug. No blood bond came from that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23908-kael03","name":"Kael03","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"230"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-08T13:31:26-07:00","id":1401749,"title":"","content":"Cool thanks for the comments all!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26213-jrichview","name":"jrichview","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26213_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"18"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-09T00:52:42-07:00","id":1401871,"title":"","content":"V5 is very clear about the blood bond: it activates only when someone drinks DIRECTLY from a Vampire. If the blood is poured into some sort of container instead, a cup or something like that, the vitae can be drunk without incurring the blood bond. This works nicely for your purposes and helps certain predator types avoid enslavement simply due to their dietary preferences...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24992-haquim","name":"Haquim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24992_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-09T01:55:51-07:00","id":1401874,"title":"","content":"Though that is counter to prior editions where you could sustain a blood bond through a container (Though the blood spoiled in most mundane containers if left long enough, you could certainly put blood in someone's wine to bond them if they drank it within at the least an hour, I'm pretty sure hiding blood within things is how most vampires are supposed to blood bond mortals without endangering the masquerade) the only thing blood in magical containers couldn't really do is embrace. <br /> <br /> There are no predator types that encourage enslavement. The kindred-blood vampire just needs to kill his victims or space his attacks.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-09T04:13:17-07:00","id":1401887,"title":"","content":"Considering that a Blood Point (or Rouse Check) is roughly 500ml of blood, if a single dose is a (possibly diluted) 10ml then the Blood Bond is probably not an issue unless people are doing four doses a day or so, and ghouldom is almost certainly a non-issue. You might not get much of a high from such a small dose, but you can pay around with both dosage sizes and mixing vitae.<br /> <br /> On the other hand I do like the idea of a kindred who blood bonds their herd by distributing a Blood Point or two between them per night. So it's certainly role that if a Blood Point's worth of vitae from a single kindred is consumed over a week/month then the Blood Bond forms or strengthened.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:blue\">Blue is sarcasm.</span><br /> <br /> If I suggestion I make contradicts in-setting metaphysics please ignore me, I probably brought in scientific ideas.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4507-twodsix","name":"TwoDSix","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"833"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}